window.DOCS_DATA = {
  "ad-network": {
    "name": "ad-network",
    "filename": "ad-network.md",
    "content": "# Ad Network Manager\n\nThe Ad Network Manager is an **optional**, self-hosted advertising server that\nruns inside PhoneServer. It is a separate HTTP server on its **own port**\n(`8090`), with its own SQLite database, its own files and its own account\nsystem. It never touches the port-8080 file server, its data or its password.\n\nIt is off until the owner enables it, and it works without any cloud service,\nad exchange, third-party SDK or external database. A site embeds one small\nscript from the phone; the phone chooses an approved creative, counts real\nimpressions/clicks and keeps the billing ledger.\n\n- [1. What it is](#1-what-it-is)\n- [2. Getting started](#2-getting-started)\n- [3. Roles and workflow](#3-roles-and-workflow)\n- [4. Sites and placements](#4-sites-and-placements)\n- [5. Campaigns, creatives and targeting](#5-campaigns-creatives-and-targeting)\n- [6. Embedding the SDK](#6-embedding-the-sdk)\n- [7. Counting, fraud and billing](#7-counting-fraud-and-billing)\n- [8. Dashboard](#8-dashboard)\n- [9. HTTP API](#9-http-api)\n- [10. Data, retention and backups](#10-data-retention-and-backups)\n- [11. Security and privacy](#11-security-and-privacy)\n- [12. Limits and troubleshooting](#12-limits-and-troubleshooting)\n- [13. Source files](#13-source-files)\n\n---\n\n## 1. What it is\n\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Default port | **8090** (`AdNetworkManager.PORT`) |\n| Enablement | Opt-in switch in **Ad Network** settings; not started by default |\n| Startup | Started by `ServerService` via `AdNetworkManager.startIfEnabled()` |\n| Server | `AdHttpServer` \u2014 HTTP/1.1, one request per connection, 4\u201324 worker threads |\n| Database | `adnet.db` from `AdDb` (app-private databases directory) |\n| Preferences | `adnet_prefs` \u2014 enabled flag and the one-time setup code |\n| Creative images | `filesDir/adnet/creatives/` (`AdDelivery.creativesDir`) |\n| Dashboard | `GET /` on port 8090 (noindex, strict CSP) |\n| Public endpoints | `/sdk.js`, `/serve`, `/imp`, `/c`, `/media/<id>`, `/health` |\n| Accounts | Internal admin / advertiser / publisher accounts (email + password) |\n| Currency model | Integer **micros** (1 unit = 1,000,000 micros); currency label defaults to `USD` |\n\nThe ad server has a deliberately different auth model from the file server:\nthere is no access-password gate on ad delivery, because the SDK must be\npublic. Management endpoints use bearer session tokens, and public delivery is\nprotected by placement keys, site-domain checks, signed event tokens and rate\nlimits.\n\n> The ad network is **not** a third-party advertising SDK. Ads are served from\n> the owner's phone. The documentation site itself (`web/`) never embeds an ad\n> tag, so its privacy statements about no third-party requests remain true.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Getting started\n\n1. In the app, open **Settings \u2192 Ad Network** and enable it. Start the main\n   server (port 8080) as usual; the ad server starts with it.\n2. Note the address: `http://<phone-ip>:8090` and the **setup code** shown only\n   on the phone.\n3. Open `http://<phone-ip>:8090/` in a browser on the same network.\n4. Choose **Set up administrator**, enter the setup code, then an email,\n   display name, company (optional) and a password (10\u2013128 characters with at\n   least one letter and one digit).\n5. The first admin can create publisher and advertiser accounts in\n   **Users**, or enable **Allow sign-up** in **Settings** (disabled by default).\n6. A publisher registers a site and a placement, copies the tag into the site.\n   An advertiser creates a campaign, adds creatives, submits it for review. An\n   admin approves the site/campaign/creatives.\n7. Approved creatives start serving. Use **Reports**, **Billing** and\n   **Admin \u2192 Fraud** to monitor delivery.\n\nUseful checks:\n\n```bash\n# Public health probe (no auth)\ncurl http://<phone-ip>:8090/health\n\n# The SDK file is public and cacheable for an hour\ncurl -I http://<phone-ip>:8090/sdk.js\n```\n\nThe setup code is single-use: it is cleared after the first administrator is\ncreated. It can be regenerated from the app if needed. `/api/setup/status`\nreports `{\"needs_setup\":true|false}` so the dashboard can show the correct\nfirst-run screen.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Roles and workflow\n\n| Role | Can do |\n|---|---|\n| **admin** | Everything: users, credits/payouts, approve/reject sites and campaigns, view all reports, fraud review, platform settings |\n| **publisher** | Register sites and placements, fetch embed tags, view own site/placement reports and earnings |\n| **advertiser** | Create campaigns and creatives, upload images, submit for review, view own campaign reports and spend |\n\nTypical approval flow:\n\n```\npublisher: register site \u2500\u2500\u25ba admin approves site\npublisher: create placement \u2500\u2500\u25ba copy tag\nadvertiser: create campaign (draft) \u2500\u2500\u25ba add creatives \u2500\u2500\u25ba submit\nadmin: approve campaign (optionally approves pending creatives)\nadvertiser: pause/resume as needed \u2500\u2500\u25ba delivery starts/stops\n```\n\n- A new site created by a publisher is `pending`; an admin-created site is\n  `approved` immediately. Delivery only selects placements whose site is\n  `approved` and placement is `active`.\n- Campaigns are editable only in `draft`, `rejected` or `paused` state. Any\n  creative edit sends it back to `pending` review, so an approved ad cannot be\n  swapped for different content.\n- Rejected campaigns/creatives carry a `reject_reason`, which publishers and\n  advertisers can see in their own records.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Sites and placements\n\nA **site** is a registered domain, owned by one publisher (or the admin). A\n**placement** is a named slot on that site with a format and, for banners, a\nsize.\n\n### Site fields\n\n| Field | Rules |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | 1\u201380 characters |\n| `domain` | Normalised hostname such as `example.com`; scheme, path, port and `www.` are stripped; subdomains match at delivery |\n| limit | 50 active sites per owner |\n| uniqueness | One active registration per domain |\n\n`AdSecurity.normalizeDomain` accepts a real hostname (labels with letters,\ndigits and hyphens) or exactly `localhost`. **Bare IP addresses are rejected**\n\u2014 register a hostname even for LAN testing.\n\n### Placement fields\n\n| Field | Rules |\n|---|---|\n| `site_id` | Must belong to the caller (admins may target any site) |\n| `name` | 1\u201380 characters |\n| `format` | `banner`, `native` or `text` |\n| `width` / `height` | Required for `banner`, 1\u20132000 px each |\n| limit | 100 active placements per site |\n| `key` | Generated `pl_` + 16 hex characters, unique and non-secret |\n\nCommon banner sizes used by the dashboard: 300\u00d7250, 728\u00d790, 320\u00d750, 300\u00d7600,\n160\u00d7600, 970\u00d7250, 336\u00d7280, 468\u00d760 and 320\u00d7100. Any dimensions in range are\naccepted, but the creative must match the placement exactly for banners.\n\n`GET /api/placements/:id/tag` returns:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tag\": \"<div data-adnet-placement=\\\"pl_0123456789abcdef\\\"></div>\\n<script async src=\\\"http://192.168.1.50:8090/sdk.js\\\"></script>\",\n  \"base_url\": \"http://192.168.1.50:8090\",\n  \"https_warning\": true\n}\n```\n\n`base_url` comes from the admin **public_base_url** setting, falling back to\nthe request `Host` header. Set it explicitly to the HTTPS origin when the\nhosting page is HTTPS; otherwise the browser blocks the script as mixed\ncontent.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Campaigns, creatives and targeting\n\n### Campaign fields\n\n| Field | Rules |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | 1\u2013100 characters |\n| `pricing` | `CPM` or `CPC` |\n| `bid` | Decimal \u2265 0.001; CPM is per 1000 impressions, CPC is per click |\n| `total_budget` | > 0 |\n| `daily_budget` | Optional; 0 = no daily cap; cannot exceed total |\n| `start` / `end` | ISO/date strings parsed by the dashboard; end is required and must be after start |\n| `targeting` | JSON object (see below) |\n| `advertiser_id` | Admin only; must reference an advertiser account |\n\nBudget and balance are checked at delivery. A campaign stops serving when its\ntotal budget is exhausted; it also requires the advertiser's prepaid balance\nto be positive. An admin funds advertisers with **credit**; publishers are paid\nwith **payout** ledger entries.\n\n### Creative fields\n\n| Field | Rules |\n|---|---|\n| `campaign_id` | Must belong to the caller (admins may target any campaign) |\n| `type` | `banner`, `native` or `text` |\n| `title` | Required for native/text; max 60 (native) or 70 (text/banner) |\n| `body` | Required for native/text; max 140 (native) or 120 (text/banner) |\n| `cta` | Optional, max 24 characters |\n| `click_url` | Required, full `http://` or `https://` URL, max 2048, no credentials or whitespace |\n| `width` / `height` | Required for banners and must match the placement |\n| limit | 30 creatives per campaign |\n| image | PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP, \u2264 2 MB, \u2264 4000\u00d74000 px, exact banner dimensions |\n\nImages are uploaded as a raw body to `POST /api/creatives/:id/image` and are\nserved only by database id (`/media/<creativeId>`), never by a client-supplied\nfile path. SVG is rejected. Files are content-sniffed rather than trusting the\nfilename or `Content-Type`.\n\n### Targeting keys\n\n`targeting` accepts only these keys; unknown keys are dropped:\n\n| Key | Type / rules |\n|---|---|\n| `devices` | Up to 3 of `mobile`, `tablet`, `desktop` |\n| `languages` | Up to 20 two-letter codes, e.g. `en` |\n| `sites` | Up to 50 site ids |\n| `hours` | 0\u201323 |\n| `weekdays` | 1\u20137, Monday = 1 |\n| `url_contains` | Up to 10 lower-case words using letters, digits and `. _ - /` |\n| `url_excludes` | Up to 10, same character rules |\n| `freq_cap_per_day` | 0\u2013100; 0 = no cap |\n\nTargeting is evaluated against the page URL (without query/fragment, lower-\ncased and truncated), the device parsed from the user agent, the browser\nlanguage prefix, the local hour/weekday and the placement's site. Geo targeting\nis intentionally absent: the app has no IP-location database, so it does not\npretend to geotarget.\n\nSelection is a weighted random rotation among eligible creatives using effective\nCPM (for CPC, the smoothed expected value is `bid \u00d7 (clicks + 2) / (imps + 200)\n\u00d7 1000`). Campaigns with many creatives do not get extra weight. If nothing is\neligible, `/serve` answers **204 No Content**; it never substitutes a fake ad.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Embedding the SDK\n\nMinimal tag (one placement per page or several):\n\n```html\n<div data-adnet-placement=\"pl_0123456789abcdef\"></div>\n<script async src=\"http://192.168.1.50:8090/sdk.js\"></script>\n```\n\nThe script finds every `[data-adnet-placement]` element and requests\n`/serve?p=<key>&u=<page>&l=<language>`. It creates the ad markup (image for\nbanners, title/body/CTA for native/text), opens the click-through through `/c`\nand sends a viewable impression to `/imp` once the ad has been **at least 50%\nvisible for one second**. The self-contained form\n`data-adnet-placement` with `data-placement` is also supported by the SDK.\n\nRequirements and gotchas:\n\n- The request needs an `Origin` or `Referer`; the host must match the site's\n  registered domain (subdomains allowed). A bare IP page cannot pass this\n  check \u2014 use a hostname.\n- HTTPS pages must use an HTTPS ad-server base URL (`public_base_url`), or the\n  browser blocks the SDK as mixed content.\n- A strict Content-Security-Policy on the hosting page must allow the ad\n  server in `script-src`, `img-src` and `connect-src`.\n- The tag is not secret and does not include credentials. Abuse is limited by\n  the domain check, placement key, signed event tokens and rate limits.\n- Do not embed the tag on the documentation site: `web/` promises no\n  third-party requests and no tracking.\n\nPublic delivery headers include permissive CORS (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin:\n*`), `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin` and `X-Content-Type-Options:\nnosniff`. Creative media is additionally sandboxed with `Content-Security-Policy:\ndefault-src 'none'; sandbox`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Counting, fraud and billing\n\nEvery counted event must correspond to a real HTTP request carrying a\nserver-signed token. Nothing is simulated.\n\n- `/serve` issues an impression token: `v1|nonce|campaign|creative|placement|\n  site|issued|visitor`, HMAC-SHA256 signed with a per-install random secret.\n- Impression tokens are valid for **6 hours**; click tokens for **24 hours**.\n  A token can be used **once**; replays are recorded as invalid traffic.\n- Impressions count only after the SDK's viewability rule (\u226550% for 1s).\n- Clicks require a prior valid impression for the same nonce, otherwise they\n  are recorded as `no_impression`/`invalid_impression`.\n- Billing is `micros`: CPM costs `bid / 1000` per valid impression; CPC costs\n  the full `bid` per valid click. The publisher share is `rev_share_pct`\n  (default **70%**). When a valid event is billed, the campaign's\n  `spent_micros` and the advertiser's `balance_micros` are updated in the same\n  transaction and the event stores both `cost_micros` and `pub_micros`.\n- Invalid events are still stored (with `valid=0` and a `reason`) so reports\n  show invalid traffic, but they are **never billed** and never earn the\n  publisher.\n\nInvalid-traffic rules (`AdFraud`):\n\n| Rule | Trigger |\n|---|---|\n| Bot user agent | Missing/short UA or a known bot/crawler/headless/HTTP-client signature |\n| Domain mismatch | Origin/Referer host does not belong to the placement's site |\n| Impression flood | More than 120 impressions/min/IP |\n| Visitor cap | More than 50 valid impressions/day/visitor/campaign (configurable) |\n| Click flood | More than 10 clicks/min/IP (configurable) |\n| Click too fast | Less than 500 ms between impression and click (configurable) |\n| Repeat clicks | More than 3 valid clicks/hour/visitor/campaign (configurable) |\n\nAdmins can review flagged sites. `AdFraud.reviewSites` flags (never auto-bans) a\nsite whose 48-hour click-through rate exceeds 15% (with \u2265100 impressions) or\nwhose invalid-traffic share exceeds 40% (with \u226550 events).\n\n### Privacy-related delivery behaviour\n\n- `DNT: 1` or `Sec-GPC: 1` suppresses the visitor id: no frequency capping or\n  per-visitor fraud check for that request. It does not suppress the ad itself.\n- The visitor id is a daily-rotating HMAC of `IP + User-Agent`; no raw IP is\n  stored with the visitor id, no cookie is set, and the id cannot be correlated\n  across days.\n- Bots receive **204** and no ad.\n- No fill returns **204**; no house/fake ad is inserted.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Dashboard\n\nOpen `http://<phone-ip>:8090/`. It is a self-contained static app\n(`dashboard.html`, `dashboard.js`, `dashboard.css`) with a strict CSP:\n\n```\ndefault-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob: data:; style-src 'self';\nscript-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none';\nbase-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'\n```\n\nConsequently the dashboard has no inline scripts, inline styles or inline event\nhandlers; icons are inline SVG built in JavaScript. It is `noindex, nofollow`\nand requires JavaScript (a `<noscript>` panel explains this).\n\nMain areas:\n\n- **Overview** \u2014 real totals, daily trend, top campaigns/sites.\n- **Sites / Placements** \u2014 register sites, create placements and copy tags.\n- **Campaigns / Creatives** \u2014 create, edit, submit, approve/reject, upload\n  images, pause/resume.\n- **Reports** \u2014 summary and breakdown (`campaign`, `site`, `placement`,\n  `creative`, `day`, `country` where applicable) with CSV export.\n- **Users** \u2014 admin-only; create/disable users, credit advertisers, record\n  publisher payouts and delete users.\n- **Billing** \u2014 ledger lines plus daily spend/earnings.\n- **Admin** \u2014 platform settings, fraud review, overview.\n\nThe dashboard uses a session bearer token held by the browser. The API is\nsame-origin only (`connect-src 'self'`), so the token is not exposed to other\norigins.\n\n### Deleting a user\n\nAdmins can delete an advertiser or publisher from **Users**. This is permanent:\n\n- the account cannot delete itself;\n- the **last administrator** cannot be deleted;\n- the user's `stats_daily` and `events` rows are purged first (for owned\n  campaigns/sites);\n- the user row is deleted, cascading to sites, placements, campaigns,\n  creatives, billing rows and sessions;\n- the deleted creative image files are removed (after IDs are looked up);\n- the action is recorded in the audit log as `user.delete` with the email and\n  id.\n\nIf you only want to block access, **disable** the account instead; disabling\nalso invalidates its sessions.\n\n---\n\n## 9. HTTP API\n\nBase URL: `http://<phone-ip>:8090`. All `/api/*` management endpoints return\nJSON and are same-origin in the dashboard. Authentication is\n`Authorization: Bearer <session-token>` except for setup/login/signup.\n\n### Public endpoints\n\n| Method | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/health` | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `GET` | `/sdk.js` | JavaScript SDK (cache 1h) |\n| `GET` | `/serve?p=<key>&u=<page>&l=<lang>` | Choose an ad; 204 on no fill |\n| `POST` | `/imp?t=<token>` | Viewable impression beacon; 204 |\n| `GET` | `/c?t=<token>` | Count click, then 302 to the creative URL |\n| `GET` | `/media/<creativeId>` | Approved creative image |\n| `OPTIONS` | public paths | CORS preflight |\n\n### Auth and account\n\n| Method | Path | Role | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/setup/status` | public | Whether the first admin is needed |\n| `POST` | `/api/setup` | public | Create first admin with the phone's setup code |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/login` | public | Email + password \u2192 `{token,user}` |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/signup` | public if enabled | Self-service advertiser/publisher |\n| `GET` | `/api/auth/me` | any | Current user |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/logout` | any | Revoke the current session |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/password` | any | Change password (revokes other sessions) |\n| `GET` | `/api/meta` | any | Currency, rev share, signup flag, base URL |\n\nPasswords use PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 60,000 iterations and a random salt;\nsessions are 12-hour random bearer tokens stored as SHA-256 hashes. Login\nattempts are limited per IP+email and per IP; unknown emails still run a dummy\nhash so timing cannot reveal account existence.\n\n### Users (admin only)\n\n| Method | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/users?role=` | List users; `role` optional |\n| `POST` | `/api/users` | Create `{email,name,company,password,role}` |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/users/:id` | Update name/company/status/password |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/users/:id` | Delete user and owned data (see \u00a78) |\n| `POST` | `/api/users/:id/credit` | Top up/adjust an advertiser balance |\n| `POST` | `/api/users/:id/payout` | Record a publisher payout |\n\nCredit body: `{\"kind\":\"topup\"|\"adjustment\",\"amount\":10.00,\"debit\":false,\n\"note\":\"...\"}`. Payout body: `{\"amount\":5.00,\"note\":\"...\"}`; it cannot exceed\nunpaid earnings and only applies to publishers.\n\n### Sites and placements\n\n| Method | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/sites` | List visible sites |\n| `POST` | `/api/sites` | Create `{name,domain}` |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/sites/:id` | Rename/re-domain (admin can set status/flags) |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/sites/:id` | Soft-remove site and its placements |\n| `GET` | `/api/placements?site_id=` | List placements |\n| `POST` | `/api/placements` | Create `{site_id,name,format,width,height}` |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/placements/:id` | Rename or set `active`/`paused` |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/placements/:id` | Soft-remove placement |\n| `GET` | `/api/placements/:id/tag` | Return embed tag and base URL |\n\n### Campaigns and creatives\n\n| Method | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/campaigns` | List visible campaigns |\n| `POST` | `/api/campaigns` | Create campaign |\n| `GET` | `/api/campaigns/:id` | Campaign detail |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/campaigns/:id` | Edit editable campaigns |\n| `POST` | `/api/campaigns/:id/submit` | Submit for review |\n| `POST` | `/api/campaigns/:id/approve` | Admin approve (optional `approve_creatives`) |\n| `POST` | `/api/campaigns/:id/reject` | Admin reject with `reason` |\n| `POST` | `/api/campaigns/:id/pause` | Pause an approved campaign |\n| `POST` | `/api/campaigns/:id/resume` | Resume a paused campaign |\n| `GET` | `/api/creatives?campaign_id=` | List creatives |\n| `POST` | `/api/creatives` | Create creative |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/creatives/:id` | Edit text/URL (returns to pending) |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/creatives/:id` | Delete creative and its image |\n| `POST` | `/api/creatives/:id/image` | Raw image upload (\u22642 MB) |\n| `GET` | `/api/creatives/:id/image` | Fetch the image (owner/admin) |\n| `POST` | `/api/creatives/:id/approve` | Admin approve |\n| `POST` | `/api/creatives/:id/reject` | Admin reject with `reason` |\n\nExample: create a publisher site and a banner placement.\n\n```bash\nBASE=http://192.168.1.50:8090\nTOKEN=...   # from /api/auth/login\n\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/api/sites\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"name\":\"My blog\",\"domain\":\"blog.example.com\"}'\n\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/api/placements\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"site_id\":1,\"name\":\"Sidebar\",\"format\":\"banner\",\"width\":300,\"height\":250}'\n\ncurl -s \"$BASE/api/placements/1/tag\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\"\n```\n\n### Reports and billing\n\n| Method | Path | Parameters |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/reports/summary` | `from`, `to`, `campaign_id`, `site_id` |\n| `GET` | `/api/reports/breakdown` | above + `by` (`campaign`, `site`, `placement`, `creative`, `day`) |\n| `GET` | `/api/reports/export` | same as breakdown; returns CSV download |\n| `GET` | `/api/billing` | advertisers: own ledger/spend; publishers: own earnings; admin: all |\n\nDates are `YYYY-MM-DD` in UTC. The default range is the last 30 days; ranges\nare capped at 366 days. Breakdown is capped at 500 rows ordered by impressions.\nAdvertisers never see publisher earnings; publishers never see advertiser spend\nor campaign identities.\n\n### Admin\n\n| Method | Path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/admin/overview` | Platform totals |\n| `GET` | `/api/admin/audit?limit=50` | Recent administrator activity (maximum 200) |\n| `GET` | `/api/admin/fraud` | Invalid-traffic summary and flagged sites |\n| `GET` | `/api/admin/settings` | Current platform settings |\n| `PUT` | `/api/admin/settings` | Update platform settings |\n\nSettings keys: `rev_share_pct`, `currency`, `public_base_url`, `allow_signup`,\n`trust_proxy`, `fraud_min_click_ms`, `fraud_repeat_clicks_hour`,\n`fraud_ip_clicks_min`, `fraud_max_imps_visitor_day`, `retention_days`.\n\n`trust_proxy` is off by default. When enabled, the client IP is taken from the\n**last** `X-Forwarded-For` entry, which is the value appended by the nearest\nproxy; never enable it when clients can set that header directly.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Data, retention and backups\n\n`adnet.db` lives in the app's private databases directory and contains:\n\n| Table | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `users` | Accounts, PBKDF2 password hash, role, status, prepaid balance (micros) |\n| `sessions` | SHA-256 hashes of bearer tokens with expiry |\n| `sites` | Domains, owner, moderation status, fraud flag/reason |\n| `placements` | Format/size, public placement key, status |\n| `campaigns` | Budget/pricing, spend, schedule, targeting JSON, review status |\n| `creatives` | Text/URL, image metadata, review status |\n| `events` | Every impression/click, validity, reason, cost and publisher share |\n| `stats_daily` | Aggregated daily metrics per campaign/creative/placement/site |\n| `fraud_log` | Daily counts per invalid-traffic reason |\n| `billing` | Top-ups, adjustments, payouts and resulting balances |\n| `audit` | Admin actions such as setup, create/delete user, approvals |\n| `settings` | Platform configuration and the HMAC secret |\n\nA background sweeper runs once a minute while the ad server is running:\n\n- deletes expired sessions and expired rate-limit buckets;\n- reviews sites for fraud every 10 minutes;\n- every 60 minutes, deletes events older than `retention_days` (default 30),\n  deletes fraud-log rows older than 90 days, ends campaigns whose `end_at` has\n  passed, and removes creative image files with no database row (older than one\n  hour, so in-flight uploads are safe).\n\nBackups are **not** included in the `/api/v1` project-backup system. To back\nup the ad network, copy `adnet.db` (plus its `-wal`/`-shm` sidecars while the\nserver is stopped) and `filesDir/adnet/creatives/` from an app-private backup\nor rooted file manager. The `web/UPDATE_MANIFEST.txt` archive is source code,\nnot runtime data.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Security and privacy\n\n- **Separate surface.** Port 8090, database, files, thread pool and prefs are\n  independent of the port-8080 server. Enabling ads cannot weaken file-server\n  authentication.\n- **Management auth.** Bearer sessions expire after 12 hours; logout deletes\n  the session. Admin-only routes check the role server-side. Password changes\n  revoke all other sessions.\n- **Setup.** The first admin requires the one-time code shown on the phone, and\n  setup attempts are rate-limited.\n- **Delivery checks.** Placement key format, site approval, placement active\n  status, `Origin`/`Referer` domain match, bot filtering, signed single-use\n  tokens, token expiry and event rate limits.\n- **Input validation.** Email/domain/URL validation, length limits, numeric\n  bounds, strict image sniffing and dimension checks, SQL with bound parameters\n  (the only string interpolation is from a fixed allow-list of report\n  groupings).\n- **Framing and headers.** Header values are stripped of CR/LF, the dashboard\n  sends a restrictive CSP plus `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, all responses send\n  `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and API responses are `no-store`.\n- **Privacy.** No cookies, no raw-IP visitor storage, daily-rotating visitor\n  hash, DNT/GPC honoured for visitor tracking, no third-party requests and no\n  cloud component.\n- **Known limitations.** HTTP is plaintext on the LAN; there is no TLS in the\n  app, no email verification/password reset, no CAPTCHA, and no guarantee that\n  a determined attacker cannot generate invalid traffic. Treat it as a\n  small/private ad server, not an ad exchange. For anything beyond a trusted\n  network, terminate TLS at a reverse proxy and keep port 8090 off the public\n  internet.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Limits and troubleshooting\n\n| Limit | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Request header line / count | 8 KiB / 64 |\n| Buffered `/api/*` body | 128 KiB |\n| Creative image upload | 2 MiB (server hard cap 3 MiB) |\n| Sites per owner | 50 |\n| Placements per site | 100 |\n| Creatives per campaign | 30 |\n| Report range | 366 days |\n| Breakdown rows | 500 |\n| Serve rate | 90/min/IP |\n| Impression beacon rate | 300/min/IP |\n| Click rate | 60/min/IP |\n| Management API rate | 300/min/user |\n| Event retention | 30 days (configurable) |\n\nCommon causes:\n\n| Symptom | Check |\n|---|---|\n| Tag shows nothing | Site approved? Placement active? Campaign approved and funded? Creative approved? |\n| `403 Domain not allowed` | Page host vs. registered domain; bare IPs cannot match |\n| `204` responses | No eligible campaign/creative (budget, schedule, targeting, no fill) |\n| SDK blocked on HTTPS page | Set `public_base_url` to an HTTPS ad-server URL and update the tag |\n| Browser blocks the script | Hosting page CSP must allow the ad-server origin |\n| Ads serve but no impressions | Viewability rule (\u226550% for 1s), or DNT/GPC suppressing visitor tracking only |\n| Login fails | Password policy, account disabled, rate-limited after repeated attempts |\n| Reports missing today | Counters aggregate by UTC day; today's partial row appears after the first event |\n| Dashboard broken | JavaScript disabled, or a modified asset violating the CSP (no inline code/styles) |\n\n---\n\n## 13. Source files\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `AdNetworkManager.java` | Opt-in lifecycle, port 8090, setup code, sweeper |\n| `AdHttpServer.java` | HTTP/1.1 parsing/routing, limits, CSP and headers |\n| `AdApi.java` | Management API, validation, account/site/campaign/creative logic |\n| `AdDelivery.java` | `/sdk.js`, `/serve`, `/imp`, `/c`, `/media`, selection and billing |\n| `AdSecurity.java` | PBKDF2 passwords, sessions, HMAC tokens, visitor hash, validation |\n| `AdDb.java` | Schema, settings defaults, query helpers |\n| `AdReports.java` | Summary/breakdown/CSV/billing queries and role scoping |\n| `AdFraud.java` | Invalid-traffic rules and site flagging |\n| `AdHttp.java` | Request/response objects, JSON helpers, status codes |\n| `AdRateLimiter.java` | In-memory sliding-window limits |\n| `assets/adnet/dashboard.html` | Dashboard shell (strict CSP, `<noscript>`) |\n| `assets/adnet/dashboard.js` | Dashboard application, SVG icons, API client |\n| `assets/adnet/dashboard.css` | Dashboard styles, responsive drawer navigation |\n| `assets/adnet/sdk.js` | Public embed script and viewability beacon |\n\nRelated documentation: [API.md](API.md) (port 8080 data API),\n[architecture.md](architecture.md), [storage.md](storage.md),\n[security.md](security.md), [testing.md](testing.md) and\n[platform-compatibility.md](platform-compatibility.md).\n"
  },
  "API": {
    "name": "API",
    "filename": "API.md",
    "content": "# Data & storage API (`/api/v1`)\n\nThe local SQL + file storage platform is exposed as a versioned JSON API. This\ndocument is the complete reference. It is self-contained; the in-app\n**ApiDocsActivity** screen shows a short summary.\n\n- **Base URL:** `http://<device-ip>:8080/api/v1`\n- **Content type:** requests and responses are `application/json; charset=utf-8`\n  unless a binary resource is streamed.\n- **Errors:** `{\"error\":\"<message>\",\"status\":<code>}` with a meaningful HTTP\n  status code.\n- **Port:** fixed at `8080` (`ServerService.PORT`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Authentication\n\nEvery endpoint under `/api/v1` requires authentication **except**\n`GET /health` (public liveness probe) and `OPTIONS` (CORS preflight).\n\nTwo credential kinds are accepted:\n\n### 1.1 Access password (admin)\n\nGrants admin rights to all projects. Supply it in any one of these ways:\n\n| Method | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Header | `X-Auth-Password: my-secret` |\n| Query parameter | `?pw=my-secret` |\n| HTTP Basic | `Authorization: Basic base64(user:my-secret)` (only the password part is checked) |\n\nIf **no password is configured** on the device, anonymous admin access is\nrefused \u2014 you must use a token.\n\n### 1.2 API token (scoped)\n\n```http\nAuthorization: Bearer pst_3f9c\u2026 \n```\n\nTokens are created in the app (or via `POST /tokens` by an admin). They can be:\n\n- **global** (access every project) or **project-scoped** (one project only);\n- **read-write** or **read-only**.\n\nOnly the SHA-256 hash of a token is stored; the plaintext (`pst_` + 64 hex\ncharacters) is returned **once** at creation and cannot be shown again.\nRevoking a token takes effect immediately.\n\nA project-scoped admin request cannot create projects. A read-only credential\nis refused on every mutating route (including SQL write statements).\n\n### 1.3 Failure responses\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `401 Unauthorized` | Missing/invalid credential. Adds `WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm=\"PhoneServer\"` |\n| `403 Forbidden` | Valid credential but not allowed (wrong project, read-only, non-admin) |\n| `429 Too Many Requests` | Per-IP rate limit exceeded (`Retry-After: 60`) |\n\n### 1.4 Rate limiting\n\nRequests are limited per client IP using the configured\n`api_rate_limit_per_minute` (default **240**/min; minimum 10). Exceeding it\nreturns `429`.\n\n### 1.5 CORS\n\nThe allowed origins come from the app's `allowed_origins` setting (default\n`*`). If set to a comma-separated list, only matching `Origin` headers receive\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. An empty value disables CORS headers entirely.\n\nPreflight (`OPTIONS`) returns `204` with:\n\n```\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type, X-Auth-Password\nAccess-Control-Max-Age: 600\n```\n\nCredentials are never advertised via `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Endpoints\n\n### 2.1 Health (public)\n\n#### `GET /health`\n\n```json\n{\"status\":\"online\",\"apiVersion\":\"v1\",\"server\":\"phoneserver\",\"time\":\"2026-02-04T01:55:12Z\"}\n```\n\n### 2.2 Index (authenticated)\n\n#### `GET /` (i.e. `/api/v1`)\n\nReturns the server name/version and the list of endpoint patterns.\n\n### 2.3 System (admin only)\n\n#### `GET /system`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"projects\": {\"count\": 2},\n  \"databases\": {\"count\": 3, \"bytes\": 94208},\n  \"files\": {\"count\": 12, \"bytes\": 5242880},\n  \"storage\": {\"usedBytes\": 5337088, \"freeBytes\": 10737418240, \"totalBytes\": 32000000000},\n  \"uptimeMillis\": 3600000,\n  \"apiVersion\": \"v1\"\n}\n```\n\n### 2.4 Tokens (admin only)\n\n| Method & path | Body | Success |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET /tokens` | \u2014 | `200 {\"tokens\":[\u2026]}` |\n| `POST /tokens` | `{\"name\":\"ci\",\"projectId\":\"<id-or-null>\",\"canWrite\":true}` | `201` (see below) |\n| `DELETE /tokens/{id}` | \u2014 | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n\nToken list item:\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"a1b2\u2026\",\"name\":\"ci\",\"projectId\":null,\"canWrite\":true,\n \"createdAt\":1759000000000,\"lastUsedAt\":0,\"revoked\":false}\n```\n\nCreation response (the only time the plaintext token is returned):\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"a1b2\u2026\",\"name\":\"ci\",\"projectId\":null,\"canWrite\":true,\n \"createdAt\":1759000000000,\"token\":\"pst_\u2026\",\n \"warning\":\"store this token now; it cannot be shown again\"}\n```\n\n`name` is required (\u2264 60 chars). `projectId` \u2014 when present and non-null \u2014 must\nexist and scopes the token.\n\n### 2.5 Projects\n\nProject object:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\":\"9c1f\u2026\",\"name\":\"Notes\",\"description\":\"\",\n  \"fileQuotaBytes\":0,\"maxUploadBytes\":0,\n  \"createdAt\":1759000000000,\"updatedAt\":1759000000000,\n  \"usage\":{\n    \"databaseCount\":1,\"fileCount\":3,\"folderCount\":1,\n    \"fileBytes\":2048,\"databaseBytes\":16384,\"backupBytes\":0\n  }\n}\n```\n\n(`fileQuotaBytes` `0` = unlimited; `maxUploadBytes` `0` = use the global\ndefault of 512 MiB.)\n\n| Method & path | Body / query | Success |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET /projects` | \u2014 | `200 {\"projects\":[\u2026]}`, filtered to projects the credential may see |\n| `POST /projects` | `{\"name\":\"Notes\",\"description\":\"\",\"fileQuotaBytes\":0,\"maxUploadBytes\":0}` | `201` project object |\n| `GET /projects/{id}` | \u2014 | `200` project object |\n| `PATCH /projects/{id}` (or `PUT`) | any subset of the fields | `200` project object |\n| `DELETE /projects/{id}` | `?confirm=<exact project name>` | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/stats` | \u2014 | `200` stats object |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/usage` | \u2014 | `200` usage object |\n\n- `name` is required, trimmed, \u2264 60 chars, no control characters; names are\n  unique (`409` on duplicate).\n- Deleting requires the exact project name as confirmation (`400` otherwise).\n- `stats`: `{projectId,databaseCount,fileCount,folderCount,fileBytes,databaseBytes,backupBytes}`.\n- `usage`: `{projectId,fileCount,fileBytes,quotaBytes,maxUploadBytes,freeBytes,totalBytes}`.\n\n### 2.6 Databases\n\nDatabase object:\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"77aa\u2026\",\"name\":\"app\",\"fileName\":\"app.db\",\"sizeBytes\":16384,\n \"createdAt\":1759000000000,\"updatedAt\":1759000000000}\n```\n\n| Method & path | Body / query | Success |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET /projects/{id}/databases` | \u2014 | `200 {\"databases\":[\u2026]} ` |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/databases` | `{\"name\":\"app\"}` | `201` database object |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/databases/{db}` | \u2014 | `200` database object + `tables:[\u2026]` |\n| `DELETE /projects/{id}/databases/{db}` | \u2014 | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/databases/{db}/tables` | \u2014 | `200 {\"tables\":[\u2026]} ` |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/databases/{db}/tables/{table}` | \u2014 | `200` table object |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/databases/{db}/query` | see 2.6.1 | `200` query result |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/databases/{db}/transaction` | see 2.6.2 | `200` transaction result |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/databases/{db}/export` | \u2014 | `200` SQLite file (download) |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/databases/{db}/import` | raw SQLite body, `?confirm=true` | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n\n- `{db}` may be the database **id or name** (name match is case-insensitive).\n- Database names: `[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*`, 1\u201364 chars; `system` and\n  `sqlite` are reserved. Max 50 databases per project.\n- Table summary: `{\"name\":\u2026,\"type\":\"table|view\",\"rowCount\":\u2026}`.\n- Table detail adds `columnCount`, `columns` and `indexes`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\":\"notes\",\"type\":\"table\",\"rowCount\":2,\"columnCount\":2,\n  \"columns\":[\n    {\"name\":\"id\",\"type\":\"INTEGER\",\"notNull\":false,\"primaryKey\":true,\"default\":null},\n    {\"name\":\"body\",\"type\":\"TEXT\",\"notNull\":true,\"primaryKey\":false,\"default\":null}\n  ],\n  \"indexes\":[{\"name\":\"idx_notes_body\",\"unique\":false,\"columns\":[\"body\"]}]\n}\n```\n\n#### 2.6.1 `POST \u2026/{db}/query`\n\n```json\n{\"sql\":\"SELECT * FROM notes WHERE id = ?\",\"params\":[1],\"limit\":100,\"offset\":0,\"timeBudgetMs\":5000}\n```\n\n- `sql` (required): a **single** statement. `SELECT`/`WITH`/`EXPLAIN`/`PRAGMA`\n  are read-only; `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE`/`REPLACE`/`CREATE`/`DROP`/`ALTER`\n  are writes and need a read-write credential.\n- `params` (optional): bound values, referenced with `?` (or `:name`/`@name`/\n  `$name`). Never interpolate.\n- `limit` (default 100, max 1000), `offset` (default 0),\n  `timeBudgetMs` (default 5000, max 30000; exceeded \u2192 `408`).\n\nResult (read):\n\n```json\n{\"isSelect\":true,\"keyword\":\"select\",\"rowsAffected\":0,\"truncated\":false,\n \"durationMs\":1,\"columns\":[\"id\",\"body\"],\"rows\":[[1,\"hello\"]],\"rowCount\":1}\n```\n\nResult (write):\n\n```json\n{\"isSelect\":false,\"keyword\":\"insert\",\"rowsAffected\":1,\"truncated\":false,\"durationMs\":0}\n```\n\nBinary SQL values are returned as `base64:<data>`. A result that hits the row\nlimit sets `\"truncated\": true`.\n\n#### 2.6.2 `POST \u2026/{db}/transaction`\n\n```json\n{\"statements\":[\n  {\"sql\":\"INSERT INTO notes(body) VALUES(?)\",\"params\":[\"a\"]},\n  {\"sql\":\"UPDATE notes SET body=? WHERE id=?\",\"params\":[\"b\",1]}\n]}\n```\n\nPlain strings are also accepted as elements. All statements run in one\ntransaction; on any error the transaction is rolled back. Response is a query\nresult with the summed `rowsAffected`. Explicit\n`BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK`/`SAVEPOINT` are rejected \u2014 this endpoint is the\ntransaction mechanism.\n\n#### 2.6.3 Export / import\n\n- `export` streams a consistent copy of the database\n  (`Content-Type: application/vnd.sqlite3`), using `VACUUM INTO` where\n  supported.\n- `import` replaces the database. It requires `?confirm=true` and a raw\n  SQLite body. The uploaded bytes are verified with `PRAGMA integrity_check`\n  and the **previous file is preserved** under `backups/` as\n  `<name>.db.pre-import-<timestamp>` before the swap. Invalid/empty files are\n  rejected (`400`).\n\n### 2.7 Files & folders\n\nFile object:\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"ff01\u2026\",\"projectId\":\"9c1f\u2026\",\"name\":\"photo.jpg\",\"folder\":\"albums\",\n \"mimeType\":\"image/jpeg\",\"sizeBytes\":2048113,\"checksum\":\"<sha256 hex>\",\n \"description\":\"\",\"tags\":\"\",\"createdAt\":1759000000000,\"updatedAt\":1759000000000,\n \"downloadPath\":\"/api/v1/projects/9c1f\u2026/files/ff01\u2026/download\"}\n```\n\n| Method & path | Body / query | Success |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET /projects/{id}/files` | optional `?folder=albums` | `200 {\"files\":[\u2026]}` |\n| `POST` / `PUT /projects/{id}/files` | see 2.7.1 | `201` file object |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/files/{fileId}` | \u2014 | `200` file object |\n| `PATCH` / `PUT /projects/{id}/files/{fileId}` | `{\"name\":\"new.jpg\",\"folder\":\"archive\"}` | `200` file object |\n| `DELETE /projects/{id}/files/{fileId}` | \u2014 | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/files/{fileId}/download` | optional `?inline=1` | file bytes |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/folders` | \u2014 | `200 {\"folders\":[{\"id\":\u2026,\"path\":\u2026,\"createdAt\":\u2026}]}` |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/folders` | `{\"path\":\"albums/2026\"}` | `201 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `DELETE /projects/{id}/folders` | `?path=albums/2026` | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n\nWithout `?folder=`, `GET \u2026/files` lists all files in the project (across\nfolders). Folders must be empty (no files and no subfolders) to delete (`409`\notherwise).\n\n#### 2.7.1 Upload\n\nTwo forms are supported.\n\n**Raw body (recommended; streamed, for large files):**\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"http://<ip>:8080/api/v1/projects/<pid>/files?folder=albums&name=photo.jpg&description=beach&tags=holiday\" \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Bearer pst_\u2026\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: image/jpeg\" \\\n     --data-binary @photo.jpg\n```\n\n`Content-Length` is **required** (otherwise `411`); the body is streamed off the\nsocket. The MIME type comes from the request `Content-Type`.\n\n**multipart/form-data** (buffered, capped):\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"http://<ip>:8080/api/v1/projects/<pid>/files\" \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Bearer pst_\u2026\" \\\n     -F \"file=@photo.jpg\"\n```\n\nQuery parameters `folder`, `name`, `description`, `tags` are optional. The\nstored file name is sanitised; the original name is kept in metadata. A SHA-256\nchecksum is computed while streaming.\n\nLimits: per-file upload limit (project override or 512 MiB default) and\nper-project storage quota both return `413`; the file-count limit returns `409`.\nBoth the declared length and the streamed byte count are checked.\n\n#### 2.7.2 Download\n\n- Streamed from disk with `Content-Length`, `Accept-Ranges: bytes`, and support\n  for `Range: bytes=\u2026` (`206 Partial Content`; `416` if unsatisfiable).\n- Served as `attachment` by default. Add `?inline=1` (or `?inline=true`) to\n  request inline rendering; this is honoured only when the MIME type is on the\n  small inline-safe allow-list (images, plain text, audio, mp4/webm, PDF).\n\n### 2.8 Backups\n\nBackup object:\n\n```json\n{\"id\":\"bc9f\u2026\",\"projectId\":\"9c1f\u2026\",\"fileName\":\"Notes-backup-20260204-015512.zip\",\n \"sizeBytes\":524288,\"includesFiles\":true,\"valid\":true,\"note\":\"\",\n \"createdAt\":1759000000000,\n \"downloadPath\":\"/api/v1/projects/9c1f\u2026/backups/bc9f\u2026/download\"}\n```\n\n| Method & path | Body / query | Success |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET /projects/{id}/backups` | \u2014 | `200 {\"backups\":[\u2026]}` |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/backups` | `{\"includeFiles\":true,\"note\":\"\"}` | `201` backup object |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/backups/import` | raw zip body, `?confirm=true` | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/backups/{backupId}` | \u2014 | `200` backup object |\n| `DELETE /projects/{id}/backups/{backupId}` | \u2014 | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/backups/{backupId}/restore` | `{\"confirm\":true}` | `200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `POST /projects/{id}/backups/{backupId}/validate` | \u2014 | `200 {\"valid\":true,\u2026manifest}` |\n| `GET /projects/{id}/backups/{backupId}/download` | \u2014 | `200` zip download |\n\n- `includeFiles` defaults to `true`.\n- A project keeps at most **25** backups; the oldest are pruned automatically.\n- `restore` and `import` require `confirm` and replace the project's databases\n  (and files when the archive includes them) only after a fully validated\n  extraction. Invalid archives return `422`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Status codes\n\n| Code | When |\n|---|---|\n| `200 OK` | Successful read/update |\n| `201 Created` | Resource created (project, database, file, backup, token) |\n| `204 No Content` | CORS preflight |\n| `400 Bad Request` | Invalid input, invalid JSON, missing confirmation |\n| `401 Unauthorized` | Missing/invalid credential |\n| `403 Forbidden` | Scope/read-only/non-admin violation, or SQL guard rejection |\n| `404 Not Found` | Unknown project/database/file/backup/token/endpoint |\n| `405 Method Not Allowed` | Wrong method for a known path |\n| `408 Request Timeout` | Query exceeded its time budget |\n| `409 Conflict` | Duplicate name, folder not empty, count limit |\n| `410 Gone` | File metadata exists but content is missing on disk |\n| `411 Length Required` | Streamed upload without `Content-Length` |\n| `413 Payload Too Large` | Buffered body cap, upload limit, or quota exceeded |\n| `422 Unprocessable Entity` | Backup archive failed validation |\n| `429 Too Many Requests` | Rate limit exceeded |\n| `500 Internal Server Error` | Unexpected server error |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n\n### 4.1 Create a project, database and table, then insert and query\n\n```bash\nBASE=http://192.168.1.50:8080/api/v1\nTOKEN=pst_xxxxxxxx\n\n# 1. Create a project\nPID=$(curl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"name\":\"Notes\"}' | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)[\"id\"])')\n\n# 2. Create a database\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/databases\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"name\":\"app\"}'\n\n# 3. Create a table (DDL is a write statement)\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/databases/app/query\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"sql\":\"CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, body TEXT)\"}'\n\n# 4. Insert with bound parameters\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/databases/app/query\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"sql\":\"INSERT INTO notes(body) VALUES(?)\",\"params\":[\"hello world\"]}'\n\n# 5. Read it back\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/databases/app/query\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"sql\":\"SELECT * FROM notes WHERE body = ?\",\"params\":[\"hello world\"]}'\n```\n\n### 4.2 A website using the API (JavaScript `fetch`)\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><title>Notes</title></head>\n<body>\n<h1>Notes</h1>\n<ul id=\"list\"></ul>\n<script>\n  // A read-only, project-scoped token embedded in the page.\n  const TOKEN = \"pst_xxxxxxxx\";\n  const BASE  = \"http://192.168.1.50:8080/api/v1\";\n  const PID   = \"9c1f\u2026\";\n\n  async function query(sql, params = []) {\n    const res = await fetch(`${BASE}/projects/${PID}/databases/app/query`, {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: { \"Authorization\": `Bearer ${TOKEN}`, \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ sql, params })\n    });\n    if (!res.ok) throw new Error((await res.json()).error);\n    return res.json();\n  }\n\n  (async () => {\n    const { rows } = await query(\"SELECT id, body FROM notes ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20\");\n    const list = document.getElementById(\"list\");\n    for (const [id, body] of rows) {\n      const li = document.createElement(\"li\");\n      li.textContent = body;\n      list.appendChild(li);\n    }\n  })();\n</script>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThe device's CORS setting must allow the page's origin (default `*` allows\nall). Embed only a **read-only** token in browser-delivered code \u2014 a\nread-write token in a web page can be read by anyone who can view the page.\n\n### 4.3 An app using the API (Kotlin/Java, HTTP)\n\n```kotlin\nval base = \"http://192.168.1.50:8080/api/v1\"\nval token = \"pst_xxxxxxxx\"\n\n// Upload a file (raw body keeps memory flat for large files)\nval file = File(\"/sdcard/DCIM/camera/IMG_0001.jpg\")\nval conn = (URL(\"$base/projects/$pid/files?folder=albums&name=IMG_0001.jpg\")\n        .openConnection() as HttpURLConnection).apply {\n    requestMethod = \"POST\"\n    doOutput = true\n    setRequestProperty(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer $token\")\n    setRequestProperty(\"Content-Type\", \"image/jpeg\")\n    setFixedLengthStreamingMode(file.length())   // sends Content-Length, streams\n}\nfile.inputStream().use { input -> conn.outputStream.use { it.write(input.readBytes()) } }\nprintln(conn.responseCode)   // 201\n```\n\n### 4.4 Backups\n\n```bash\n# Create a backup (including files)\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/backups\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"includeFiles\":true,\"note\":\"before migration\"}'\n\n# Download it\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -o notes-backup.zip \"$BASE/projects/$PID/backups/<backupId>/download\"\n\n# Validate and restore (restore replaces databases/files!)\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/backups/<backupId>/validate\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\"\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/backups/<backupId>/restore\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"confirm\":true}'\n\n# Import a backup produced on another device\ncurl -s -X POST \"$BASE/projects/$PID/backups/import?confirm=true\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" --data-binary @notes-backup.zip\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Notes on the SQL workspace\n\n- One statement per request. Use the transaction endpoint for multi-step work.\n- Values must be passed via `params`; string building is not needed and the\n  guard blocks dangerous constructs (`ATTACH`, `DETACH`, `load_extension`,\n  `writable_schema`, selected `PRAGMA`s, explicit transactions).\n- Row results are paginated (`limit`/`offset`) and time-bounded.\n- This is a local-first store, **not** a hardened multi-tenant SQL service. See\n  [security.md](security.md) for the full threat model and production\n  requirements (notably: put it behind HTTPS/VPN for any non-LAN access).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Ad Network API (port 8090)\n\nThe optional ad network is a **separate origin** with its own API and auth. It is documented in full in [ad-network.md](ad-network.md); this section is the endpoint index.\n\nBase URL: `http://<phone-ip>:8090`. Management endpoints use `Authorization: Bearer <session-token>` from `/api/auth/login` (12-hour sessions). Public delivery has no account auth and is constrained by placement keys, site domains, signed tokens and rate limits.\n\n### 6.1 Public delivery\n\n| Method | Path | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/health` | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| `GET` | `/sdk.js` | Embed script, `Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600` |\n| `GET` | `/serve?p=<key>&u=<page>&l=<lang>` | JSON ad or `204` on no fill; requires `Origin`/`Referer` |\n| `POST` | `/imp?t=<token>` | Viewable impression beacon, always `204` |\n| `GET` | `/c?t=<token>` | Counts click then `302` to `click_url` |\n| `GET` | `/media/<creativeId>` | Approved creative image |\n| `OPTIONS` | public paths | CORS preflight |\n\n### 6.2 Auth and accounts\n\n| Method | Path | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/setup/status` | public |\n| `POST` | `/api/setup` | public, phone setup code required |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/login` | public |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/signup` | public only when enabled |\n| `GET` | `/api/auth/me` | any |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/logout` | any |\n| `POST` | `/api/auth/password` | any |\n| `GET` | `/api/meta` | any |\n\n### 6.3 Management\n\n| Area | Endpoints |\n|---|---|\n| Users (admin) | `GET|POST /api/users`, `PATCH|DELETE /api/users/:id`, `POST /api/users/:id/credit`, `POST /api/users/:id/payout` |\n| Sites / placements | `GET|POST /api/sites`, `PATCH|DELETE /api/sites/:id`, `GET|POST /api/placements`, `PATCH|DELETE /api/placements/:id`, `GET /api/placements/:id/tag` |\n| Campaigns | `GET|POST /api/campaigns`, `GET|PATCH /api/campaigns/:id`, `POST /api/campaigns/:id/{submit,approve,reject,pause,resume}` |\n| Creatives | `GET|POST /api/creatives`, `PATCH|DELETE /api/creatives/:id`, `POST /api/creatives/:id/{image,approve,reject}` |\n| Reports | `GET /api/reports/{summary,breakdown,export}`, `GET /api/billing` |\n| Admin | `GET /api/admin/{overview,audit,fraud,settings}`, `PUT /api/admin/settings` |\n\nDates use `YYYY-MM-DD` UTC. Reports accept `from`, `to`, `campaign_id`, `site_id`; `/breakdown` and `/export` also accept `by` (`campaign`, `site`, `placement`, `creative`, `day`). The default range is 29 days back from `to`; the maximum is 366 days.\n\n### 6.4 Relationship to `/api/v1`\n\nNothing is shared: different port, database, files, auth and rate limiter. `/api/v1` tokens and the access password do not work on port 8090, and ad sessions do not work on port 8080. Ad-network data is not included in `/api/v1` project backups.\n"
  },
  "architecture": {
    "name": "architecture",
    "filename": "architecture.md",
    "content": "# Architecture\n\nThis document describes the local SQL + file storage platform that was added\non top of PhoneServer. It complements the high-level overview in the project\n[README](../README.md) and the request-level reference in [API.md](API.md).\n\nThe platform is deliberately small: a thin `data/` storage layer, a single\nHTTP facade (`DataApi`), and a set of Android screens that drive the same\nlayer. Nothing depends on a cloud service, an account, or an external database\nserver.\n\n## Component map\n\n```\n                       \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n   browser / app  \u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba \u2502  SimpleHttpServer (port 8080)\u2502\n                       \u2502   \u2022 parses requests          \u2502\n                       \u2502   \u2022 dispatches /api/v1       \u2502\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                       \u2502   \u2022 streams large uploads    \u2502  \u2502\n                       \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518  \u2502\n                                                        \u25bc\n                       \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                       \u2502  DataApi  (HTTP facade for /api/v1)           \u2502\n                       \u2502   \u2022 auth (password or bearer token)           \u2502\n                       \u2502   \u2022 per-project authorisation                 \u2502\n                       \u2502   \u2022 rate limiting, CORS, JSON framing         \u2502\n                       \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                        \u2502\n        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n        \u25bc              \u25bc               \u25bc               \u25bc              \u25bc\n  ProjectStore   DatabaseManager    FileStore     BackupManager   TokenStore\n        \u2502              \u2502               \u2502               \u2502              \u2502\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u25bc\n                  \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                  \u2502  SystemDatabase (system.db)\u2502        \u2502  Filesystem (root) \u2502\n                  \u2502  metadata only             \u2502        \u2502  databases/, files/ \u2502\n                  \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518        \u2502  backups/, tmp/     \u2502\n                                                        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\nSupporting classes: `StorageLayout`, `PathGuard`, `SqlGuard`, `MimeTypes`,\n`TokenUtil`, `RateLimiter`, `Records`, `DataException`, `BackupArchive`,\n`ContentStoreBridge`.\n\n## Layers\n\n1. **Transport \u2014 `SimpleHttpServer`.** The existing single-file HTTP server.\n   It was extended to (a) recognise `/api/v1` paths, (b) leave large raw\n   (non-multipart) upload bodies on the socket so `DataApi` can stream them,\n   (c) cap buffered (multipart / small) bodies, and (d) hand the request to\n   `DataApi.handle(...)`. It also adds the CORS response header using the\n   configured origins.\n\n2. **API facade \u2014 `DataApi`.** A stateless dispatcher that turns HTTP requests\n   into storage-layer calls and back into JSON. It owns authentication,\n   per-project authorisation checks, rate limiting, CORS preflight, JSON\n   parsing/rendering, and streaming of downloads and exports. It never touches\n   SQLite directly \u2014 it always goes through the store classes.\n\n3. **Stores \u2014 the `data/` package.** Each store owns one concern and is the\n   only place that reads/writes the corresponding tables/files:\n\n   | Store | Responsibility |\n   |---|---|\n   | `SystemDatabase` | Declares/creates `system.db` and its schema; provides the singleton helper |\n   | `ProjectStore` | Project lifecycle, per-project/global statistics, operation log |\n   | `DatabaseManager` | Per-project SQLite databases: create/list/delete, schema inspection, guarded query execution, transactions, export/import |\n   | `FileStore` | Streamed uploads, folders, download metadata, rename/move/delete, usage, integrity checks |\n   | `BackupManager` | Create/validate/download/restore/import project backups, retention |\n   | `TokenStore` | Issue/verify/revoke/list API tokens (hash-only persistence) |\n\n4. **Persistence.** `system.db` holds *metadata only*. Database files and stored\n   file bytes live on the filesystem under a single configurable data root\n   (see [storage.md](storage.md)). User-supplied names are never used as paths.\n\n## Request lifecycle for `/api/v1`\n\n1. `SimpleHttpServer` parses the request line, headers and (depending on the\n   route) either buffers or defers the body.\n2. If the path is under `/api/v1` it calls `DataApi.handle(context, req, out)`.\n3. `DataApi` rejects explicitly-too-large buffered bodies with `413`.\n4. `OPTIONS` is answered as a CORS preflight **before** authentication.\n5. `GET /api/v1/health` is answered publicly.\n6. Otherwise the per-IP rate limit is enforced (`429` when exceeded), then the\n   caller is authenticated.\n7. The first path segment selects a handler (`system`, `tokens`, `projects`);\n   project-scoped handlers verify the caller may touch that project.\n8. The handler calls the relevant store and renders JSON (or streams bytes).\n   `DataException` carries the intended HTTP status.\n\n## Design choices\n\n- **Metadata in SQL, content on disk.** Large media is never stored as SQL\n  blobs; only a generated storage key is. This keeps the metadata database\n  small and makes range downloads trivial.\n- **Generated ids and keys.** Project ids, database ids, file ids and file\n  storage keys are all generated (UUID-derived / random). The *id* is the\n  directory or file name on disk, so path traversal is structurally impossible\n  rather than merely filtered.\n- **Parameterised SQL + a static guard.** Ordinary data operations never build\n  SQL from user strings; the SQL workspace additionally runs every statement\n  through `SqlGuard` (single-statement only, forbidden keywords blocked,\n  read-only enforcement) and applies row limits and a time budget.\n- **Defence in depth for paths.** `PathGuard` normalises, validates and then\n  re-checks the canonicalised result is inside the expected root, even though\n  the storage layout already avoids user-controlled path segments.\n- **One connection style.** Project databases are opened with foreign keys,\n  WAL and a busy timeout. `system.db` uses WAL and cascading foreign keys so\n  deleting a project removes its metadata rows atomically while on-disk data is\n  removed first (so a failure never silently loses data).\n- **Safe error messages.** `DataException` messages are written to be returned\n  to clients; SQLite errors are passed through `safeMessage(...)`, which\n  replaces absolute paths with `<path>`.\n\n## Threading\n\n`SimpleHttpServer` handles each connection on its own thread. Stores are\nstateless apart from the `SystemDatabase` singleton and the cached\n`RateLimiter`; SQLite serialises access internally (WAL + busy timeout). The UI\nperforms storage work off the main thread and observes the same layer the API\nuses, so there is no second source of truth.\n\n## Ad network (optional)\n\nThe ad network is a second, independently started HTTP service. It does not add routes to `SimpleHttpServer`; it is owned by `AdNetworkManager` and started from `ServerService` only when the owner enables it.\n\n```\nServerService (port 8080)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 AdNetworkManager.startIfEnabled()\n          \u251c\u2500\u2500 AdHttpServer (port 8090, 4\u201324 workers, Connection: close)\n          \u2502      \u251c\u2500\u2500 AdApi       authenticated /api/*\n          \u2502      \u251c\u2500\u2500 AdDelivery  /sdk.js /serve /imp /c /media\n          \u2502      \u2514\u2500\u2500 static dashboard assets\n          \u251c\u2500\u2500 AdDb (adnet.db) + AdDelivery.creativesDir()\n          \u2514\u2500\u2500 AdNet-Sweeper (sessions, fraud review, retention, end dates)\n```\n\nDesign consequences:\n\n- **Fault and load isolation.** A slow or abusive ad request cannot occupy the file server's accept loop or its unbounded thread-per-connection handlers; the ad server uses a bounded pool and its own queue.\n- **Separate persistence.** `adnet.db` has no foreign keys into `system.db`. Project backups and the `/files` API never see ad data.\n- **Separate auth.** The file server's password/`X-Auth-Password`/Basic auth is not consulted on port 8090. Ad accounts use PBKDF2 hashes and bearer sessions.\n- **Public by necessity.** `/sdk.js`, `/serve`, `/imp`, `/c` and `/media/*` cannot require an interactive login because they run on third-party pages; they are protected by placement keys, domain matching, signed single-use tokens, bot filtering and rate limits instead.\n- **Strict dashboard CSP.** The dashboard assets are served with `default-src 'self'`, so all script/style must be external files; there are no inline handlers.\n\nFull details, including schema, selection, billing and privacy, are in [ad-network.md](ad-network.md).\n"
  },
  "platform-compatibility": {
    "name": "platform-compatibility",
    "filename": "platform-compatibility.md",
    "content": "# Platform compatibility & setup\n\nThis project targets **Android** (the app) and is built with the existing\nGradle/AGP setup. The storage layer itself is plain Java + Android SQLite and\nruns on the device.\n\n## Device / runtime requirements\n\n- Android API 24+ (unchanged from the existing project).\n- A network interface the clients can reach; the server binds port **8080**\n  (`ServerService.PORT`).\n- Full storage access (`MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE` / all-files access) is only\n  needed for the pre-existing shared-storage browsing features. The new data\n  platform defaults to an **app-private** data root and does not require it.\n\n## Build environment\n\n- JDK 21, Gradle wrapper 9.0.0, Android Gradle Plugin 8.5.2.\n- `local.properties` must point at an Android SDK\n  (`sdk.dir=/path/to/android-sdk`). It is git-ignored and intentionally **not**\n  part of the update archive.\n- Build commands:\n\n  ```bash\n  sh gradlew :app:assembleDebug\n  sh gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest\n  ```\n\n## Dependencies added\n\nOnly test dependencies were added to `app/build.gradle`:\n\n```groovy\ntestImplementation 'junit:junit:4.13.2'\ntestImplementation 'org.robolectric:robolectric:4.14.1'\n```\n\nNo new runtime (non-test) dependencies were introduced: the platform uses only\nthe Android framework, `org.json` (already used by the project) and\n`java.util.zip` (JDK). This satisfies the \"avoid unnecessary dependencies\"\nrequirement \u2014 SQLite is the device's built-in SQLite; no external database\nserver is needed.\n\n## Host architecture note (tests)\n\nRobolectric runs Android code on the host JVM. Its SQLite backend requires a\n**native** library and ships binaries only for:\n\n| OS | Architectures |\n|---|---|\n| Linux | x86_64 |\n| Windows | x86_64 |\n| macOS | x86_64, arm64 (Apple Silicon) |\n\nThere is **no Linux/aarch64** build of Robolectric's native runtime (verified\nagainst `nativeruntime-dist-compat` 1.0.16 \u2026 1.0.19) nor of its legacy\n`sqlite4java` fallback. On a Linux/aarch64 host JVM (for example an\n`aarch64` Linux container or an on-device JDK), Robolectric cannot open a\ndatabase and the DB-backed tests are skipped by\n`DataTestSupport.assumeRobolectricRuntimeSupported()`.\n\nConsequences:\n\n- **Pure-JVM tests** (path/SQL/MIME/token/rate-limit/storage-layout/backup\n  archive) run on any host, including Linux/aarch64.\n- **Robolectric tests** run on x86_64 Linux/Windows and on x86_64/arm64 macOS\n  (the usual developer/CI machines). They are skipped on Linux/aarch64 with an\n  explicit reason rather than failing.\n- The **app itself** is unaffected: on a real Android device the platform uses\n  the device's own SQLite.\n\nThe `--warning-mode all` Gradle hint and the \"Deprecated Gradle features\" notice\ncome from the existing build setup (Gradle 9 deprecations) and are unrelated to\nthis change.\n\n## Ad network runtime\n\n- The ad network is optional and uses a **second port, 8090** (`AdNetworkManager.PORT`). The main file server remains on 8080.\n- It requires no new dependency, permission or storage grant: SQLite is the device's built-in implementation, creative images live in app-private storage, and the dashboard is plain HTML/CSS/JS.\n- The dashboard needs a browser with JavaScript enabled. It is served with a strict self-only CSP, so modified assets must not introduce inline scripts/styles/handlers.\n- Client pages that embed the SDK need a **hostname** matching the registered site domain. Bare IP hosts are rejected by design; `localhost` is accepted as a special case.\n- HTTPS client pages require `public_base_url` to be an HTTPS URL for the ad server, or the browser blocks `/sdk.js` as mixed content. The page's own CSP must also allow the ad-server origin in `script-src`, `img-src` and `connect-src`.\n- Ad data is app-private (`databases/adnet.db`, `files/adnet/creatives/`) and is not covered by the `/api/v1` data-platform backup format.\n\nSee [ad-network.md](ad-network.md).\n"
  },
  "security": {
    "name": "security",
    "filename": "security.md",
    "content": "# Security model\n\nThis document is the security review for the local SQL + file storage platform.\nIt states what is protected, how, and \u2014 importantly \u2014 what is **not** protected\nso that deployments are not over-trusted.\n\n## Threat model\n\nThe intended deployment is a phone on a trusted local network, reachable by\nother devices on that network (or by the phone itself). There is no cloud\ncomponent and no multi-tenant hardening. The security goals are therefore:\n\n1. No unauthenticated administrative surface.\n2. No cross-project data access with a project-scoped credential.\n3. No filesystem escape via crafted names, folder paths or archives.\n4. No SQL escape via crafted statements or parameters.\n5. No secret leakage to clients through error messages or responses.\n6. Bounded resource use (request size, rows, time, rate).\n\n## Authentication\n\n`/api/v1` is authenticated by **either**:\n\n- **Access password** (interactive / admin). Supplied as\n  `X-Auth-Password: <pw>`, `?pw=<pw>`, or HTTP Basic auth. This grants admin\n  rights across all projects. Comparison uses a constant-time equality check\n  (`TokenUtil.constantTimeEquals`).\n- **Bearer API token** (`Authorization: Bearer pst_\u2026`). Tokens are created from\n  the app (or the `/tokens` endpoint by an admin), can be **scoped to a single\n  project** and/or marked **read-only**, and are stored **only as SHA-256\n  hashes**. The plaintext is shown exactly once at creation.\n- If **no password is configured**, anonymous admin access is refused: a token\n  is required. This avoids the \"empty password = open admin panel\" mistake.\n\n`GET /api/v1/health` is the only unauthenticated endpoint. `OPTIONS` (CORS\npreflight) is answered before auth so browsers can negotiate.\n\nOnly an **admin** (password) credential can call `/api/v1/system` and\n`/api/v1/tokens`. A project-scoped admin request may not create projects.\n\n## Authorisation & project isolation\n\n`requireProjectAccess` rejects a project-scoped token from touching any other\nproject. Combined with the storage layout \u2014 every project is a directory named\nby its generated id, and a token stores only that id \u2014 this makes cross-project\naccess structurally impossible, not just filtered by the API layer. A\nread-only token (`canWrite=false`) is refused on every mutating route via\n`requireWrite`, including the SQL workspace's write statements.\n\n## Path safety\n\n`PathGuard` is used everywhere user input could influence a path:\n\n- `normalizeRelative` rejects `..`, `.`, empty segments, and separators inside\n  segments.\n- `isValidSegment` / `sanitizeFileName` strip or replace reserved characters and\n  never return an empty name.\n- `safeResolve` / `isInside` canonicalise both the root and the candidate and\n  verify the result is inside the root.\n\nBecause ids/keys are generated and used as the actual on-disk names, these\nchecks are the second line of defence, not the first.\n\n## SQL safety\n\n`SqlGuard.inspect` runs before any statement reaches SQLite:\n\n- Exactly **one** statement per request (a top-level `;` with more than one\n  non-empty statement is rejected). Splitting is quote/comment aware.\n- Forbidden constructs are blocked: `ATTACH`, `DETACH`, `load_extension`,\n  `writable_schema`, and several dangerous `PRAGMA`s (`temp_store_directory`,\n  `data_store_directory`, `lock_status`, `database_list`).\n- Explicit transaction control (`BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK`/`SAVEPOINT`) is\n  rejected \u2014 use the transaction endpoint instead.\n- Only `SELECT`/`WITH`/`EXPLAIN`/`PRAGMA` (read) and\n  `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE`/`REPLACE`/`CREATE`/`DROP`/`ALTER` (write) are\n  allowed; write verbs are refused on a read-only credential.\n\nThe workspace is **parameterised**: `params` are bound, never interpolated.\nIdentification names used internally (`PRAGMA table_info`, `VACUUM INTO`, row\ncounting) are quoted with `\"` and doubled. Scope/statement analysis is done per\ndatabase file, and the layer only ever opens a file that is inside the\nproject's own `databases/` directory.\n\nThis is a **local-first data store, not a hardened multi-tenant SQL service.**\nThere is no per-statement sandbox beyond the guard and limits above.\n\n## Uploads & downloads\n\n- Upload limits are enforced against both the declared length and the actual\n  streamed byte count (`413` on violation).\n- Multipart bodies are buffered and capped; large raw uploads are streamed off\n  the socket so memory is not exhausted.\n- Downloads and exports stream from disk and support `Range`; `416` is returned\n  for unsatisfiable ranges.\n- Uploaded content is served as an **attachment** unless the MIME type is on a\n  small allow-list of inline-safe types (`MimeTypes.isInlineSafe`), so user\n  content cannot execute scripts in the server's origin. Responses also carry\n  `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and `Cache-Control: private, max-age=0`.\n\n## Backups\n\n- An archive is a zip with a `manifest.properties`; validation requires the\n  expected `format`/`version` and rejects any entry that is absolute, contains\n  a backslash, or is not a safe relative path.\n- Extraction is root-checked (`PathGuard`) and capped (4 GiB) to resist archive\n  bombs.\n- Restore/import replace data only after a full validated extraction into a\n  staging directory; the previous copy is preserved until the swap succeeds.\n- Backups contain the project's **databases, stored files and safe-to-export\n  `project-config.json`**. They never contain the access password, API tokens,\n  or other secrets.\n\n## Resource limits\n\n- Per-IP rate limit (`ContentStore.getApiRateLimitPerMinute`, default 240/min)\n  returns `429` with `Retry-After: 60`.\n- Buffered request bodies are capped (413 above the cap).\n- Query row limits and a time budget bound scans; exceeding the budget returns\n  `408`.\n- Row counts, database counts, file counts and backup counts are all bounded.\n\n## Error handling\n\nClient-visible messages come from `DataException` and are written to be safe.\nSQLite error text is passed through `safeMessage`, which replaces absolute\npaths with `<path>`. Unexpected exceptions return a generic\n`500 internal server error`; details are only logged on-device.\n\n## CORS\n\nCORS origins come from `ContentStore.getAllowedOrigins` (default `*`). An empty\nvalue disables CORS headers entirely (same-origin only). Credentials are\n**never** advertised via `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials`, so browsers will\nnot attach ambient credentials cross-origin.\n\n## Explicit non-goals / production requirements\n\n- **No TLS in the app.** HTTP is plaintext. For any access beyond a trusted LAN\n  you must put the server behind an HTTPS reverse proxy (or a VPN/SSH tunnel)\n  and terminate TLS there. Anything on the same network can otherwise read\n  credentials and data.\n- **The access password and tokens are bearer secrets.** Anyone who obtains\n  them has the associated scope. Tokens can be revoked from the app/API.\n- **No audit-grade logging or login throttling beyond the per-IP rate limit.**\n- **No encryption at rest.** Files and databases are stored unencrypted in the\n  app-private data root. Device-level encryption is the only protection.\n- **No protection against a malicious local app** on a rooted/compromised\n  device.\n\nDo not treat this as production security for sensitive or multi-user data.\n\n## Ad network security\n\nThe optional ad network has a separate threat model and auth system from `/api/v1`:\n\n- **Separate origin and data.** Port `8090`, `adnet.db`, creative files and `adnet_prefs`; no shared sessions, tokens or access password.\n- **Accounts.** PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (60,000 iterations, 16-byte salt), 10\u2013128 character passwords with letters and digits, 12-hour bearer sessions stored only as SHA-256 hashes, revocation on logout/password change/disable.\n- **First admin.** `/api/setup` requires a one-time code shown on the phone and is refused once any user exists; setup attempts are rate-limited.\n- **Login abuse.** Per IP+email and per-IP windows; unknown emails run a dummy PBKDF2 hash so timing cannot reveal account existence.\n- **Authorisation.** Admin-only routes are checked server-side. Publishers can only manage their sites/placements; advertisers can only manage their campaigns/creatives. Foreign ids return 404.\n- **Delivery.** `/serve` requires a valid `pl_` key, an approved site, an active placement and an `Origin`/`Referer` host matching the registered domain (subdomains allowed). `/imp` and `/c` require HMAC-signed, single-use, expiring tokens; clicks require a prior valid impression. Bots get `204`; no fill gets `204`.\n- **Invalid traffic.** Bot UA filtering, per-IP serve/beacon/click limits, visitor/day caps, minimum click delay, repeat-click caps and site flagging. Invalid events are recorded but never billed.\n- **Input handling.** Strict email/domain/URL/number validation, image content sniffing (PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP only, no SVG), exact banner dimensions, bound SQL parameters, fixed report grouping expressions.\n- **Response safety.** Header values strip CR/LF; API responses are `no-store`; every response has `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`; creative media is served with a `default-src 'none'; sandbox` CSP; the dashboard has a strict self-only CSP plus `X-Frame-Options: DENY`.\n- **Privacy.** No cookies and no raw-IP visitor storage. The visitor id is a daily-rotating HMAC of IP+UA used only for frequency capping/fraud, and `DNT: 1`/`Sec-GPC: 1` suppresses it. No third-party requests or cloud services are involved.\n\nResidual risks: plaintext HTTP on the LAN, no TLS in the app, no email verification/password reset/CAPTCHA, heuristic fraud detection, and no guarantee against determined invalid traffic. Treat this as a small private ad server, not an ad exchange. See [ad-network.md](ad-network.md) \u00a711.\n"
  },
  "storage": {
    "name": "storage",
    "filename": "storage.md",
    "content": "# Storage layout\n\nAll persistent platform data lives under a single configurable **data root**.\nNothing is ever written into build directories, the app's private files root is\nonly used as a fallback, and file contents are kept out of SQL.\n\n## Where the data root lives\n\n`ContentStore.getDataRoot(context)` resolves the root in this order:\n\n1. The path configured in **Advanced Settings** (`server_config` \u2192\n   `data_root`), if it exists and is writable.\n2. The app-private external directory `\u2026/Android/data/<applicationId>/files/phoneserver-data`.\n3. The app-private internal directory `files/phoneserver-data` (fallback when\n   external storage is unavailable).\n\nPoints 2 and 3 are app-private: not world-readable, survive app updates, and\nare removed when the app is uninstalled. Point 1 lets an advanced deployment\nplace data on shared storage.\n\nThe root is resolved once per operation via `ContentStore.getStorageLayout()`.\n\n## Directory structure\n\n```\n<root>/\n  system/\n    system.db                 # metadata only (projects, files, tokens, backups, log)\n  tmp/                        # in-progress uploads / backup scratch (.part files)\n  projects/\n    <project-id>/\n      databases/<name>.db     # one SQLite file per logical database (plus -wal/-shm)\n      files/<storage-key>     # raw uploaded bytes, named by a generated key\n      backups/<backup-id>.zip # project backup archives\n      project-config.json     # safe-to-export project configuration (no secrets)\n```\n\n- `<project-id>`, `<storage-key>` and `<backup-id>` are generated, ASCII-safe\n  tokens matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}`. They are used verbatim as path\n  segments, so a malicious *name* can never influence a path.\n- `<name>` is a validated database name (`[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*`, 1\u201364\n  chars, `system`/`sqlite` reserved). `DatabaseManager` also re-checks the\n  resolved file is inside the project's `databases/` directory.\n\n## `system.db` schema (version 1)\n\nCreated by `SystemDatabase.onCreate`. Versioning is handled by\n`onUpgrade` with additive, per-version steps.\n\n| Table | Purpose | Key columns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `projects` | Project records | `id` PK, `name` UNIQUE, `description`, `file_quota_bytes`, `max_upload_bytes`, timestamps |\n| `project_databases` | Registered databases | `id` PK, `project_id` FK \u2192 projects (cascade), `name`, `file_name`, `size_bytes` |\n| `files` | Stored-file metadata | `id` PK, `project_id` FK (cascade), `original_name`, `storage_key` UNIQUE, `folder_path`, `mime_type`, `size_bytes`, `checksum`, `description`, `tags` |\n| `folders` | Logical folders | `id` PK, `project_id` FK (cascade), `path` (unique per project) |\n| `api_tokens` | API credentials | `id` PK, `name`, `token_hash` UNIQUE, `project_id` (nullable), `can_write`, `revoked`, `last_used_at` |\n| `backups` | Backup archives | `id` PK, `project_id` FK (cascade), `file_name`, `size_bytes`, `includes_files`, `valid`, `note` |\n| `operations` | Rolling operation log | `id` autoincrement, `project_id`, `action`, `detail`, `created_at` (trimmed to the newest 500 rows) |\n\n`onConfigure` enables `PRAGMA foreign_keys`, and the helper is created with\n`setWriteAheadLoggingEnabled(true)`.\n\n## Project databases\n\nEach logical database is an independent SQLite file under\n`projects/<project-id>/databases/`. `DatabaseManager.openFile` enables foreign\nkeys, WAL and `PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000` for writable opens. New files are\ncreated with `PRAGMA auto_vacuum = INCREMENTAL`.\n\n`DatabaseManager.list` reconciles the metadata table with what is actually on\ndisk and **adopts** any `*.db` file it finds without metadata (for example\nafter a manual copy), so nothing becomes invisible.\n\nWhen a database is deleted its sidecar files (`-wal`, `-shm`, `-journal`) are\nremoved too, so re-creating a database of the same name cannot inherit stale\nstate.\n\n## Uploads and temporaries\n\nUploads are streamed into `tmp/<prefix>-<id>.part` and renamed into\n`files/<storage-key>` only after the bytes are fully written and the SHA-256\nchecksum computed. On any failure the temp file is deleted. Restores extract\ninto `tmp/restore-<id>/` and swap directories into place only after a complete,\nvalidated extraction (the previous copy is kept until the swap succeeds).\n\n## Quotas and limits\n\n| Limit | Value | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Default max upload per file | 512 MiB | `ContentStore.DEFAULT_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES` |\n| Per-project upload limit | project `maxUploadBytes` (0 \u2192 default) | `FileStore.upload` |\n| Per-project storage quota | project `fileQuotaBytes` (0 \u2192 unlimited) | `FileStore.upload` |\n| Max files per project | 20 000 | `FileStore.MAX_FILES_PER_PROJECT` |\n| Max databases per project | 50 | `DatabaseManager.MAX_DATABASES_PER_PROJECT` |\n| Max backups per project | 25 (oldest pruned) | `BackupManager.MAX_BACKUPS_PER_PROJECT` |\n| Query row limit | 100 default, 1000 max | `DatabaseManager` |\n| Query time budget | 5 s default, 30 s max | `DatabaseManager` |\n| Restore size cap | 4 GiB | `BackupArchive.DEFAULT_MAX_TOTAL_BYTES` |\n\nBoth the *declared* length and the *streamed* byte count are checked, so a\nclient cannot bypass the upload limit by lying about `Content-Length`.\n\n## Ad network data\n\nThe optional ad network keeps its data outside the project data root and outside `system.db`:\n\n```\napp private storage/\n  databases/\n    adnet.db                 # users, sessions, sites, placements, campaigns,\n                             # creatives, events, stats_daily, fraud_log,\n                             # billing, audit, settings (+ -wal/-shm)\n  files/\n    adnet/creatives/\n      <24-hex>.<png|jpg|gif|webp>\n  shared_prefs/\n    adnet_prefs.xml          # enabled flag + one-time setup code\n```\n\n| Table | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `users` | email, PBKDF2 hash, role, status, prepaid balance (micros) |\n| `sessions` | SHA-256 token hashes, user id, expiry |\n| `sites` | owner, domain, moderation status, fraud flag/reason |\n| `placements` | site, format/size, `pl_` key, status |\n| `campaigns` | advertiser, pricing/bid/budgets, schedule, targeting JSON, review status |\n| `creatives` | campaign, type/text/URL, image file/mime/dimensions, review status |\n| `events` | one row per impression/click, validity/reason, cost and publisher share |\n| `stats_daily` | daily aggregates per campaign/creative/placement/site |\n| `fraud_log` | invalid-traffic counts by day and reason |\n| `billing` | top-ups, adjustments, payouts and resulting balances |\n| `audit` | admin actions (setup, user create/delete, approvals, billing) |\n| `settings` | platform configuration and the HMAC secret |\n\nRetention is managed by the ad-network sweeper: expired sessions are deleted every minute, fraud review runs every 10 minutes, and events older than `retention_days` (default **30**) plus fraud-log days older than 90 are removed hourly. Orphaned creative images are deleted after one hour. Ad data is **not** part of `/api/v1` project backups; back up `adnet.db` and `adnet/creatives/` separately.\n\nSee [ad-network.md](ad-network.md) for delivery-count semantics and the full API.\n"
  },
  "testing": {
    "name": "testing",
    "filename": "testing.md",
    "content": "# Testing\n\n## How to run\n\nFrom the project root:\n\n```bash\n# Unit tests\nsh gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest\n\n# Debug build\nsh gradlew :app:assembleDebug\n```\n\nHTML reports are written to `app/build/reports/tests/testDebugUnitTest/`.\n\n## Frameworks\n\n- **JUnit 4** (`junit:junit:4.13.2`) for all tests.\n- **Robolectric 4.14.1** (plus `testOptions.unitTests.includeAndroidResources`)\n  for stores that need Android APIs (`Context`, `SQLiteDatabase`). No\n  instrumentation tests are used, so no emulator/device is required for the\n  parts that can run.\n\nTests live under `app/src/test/java/\u2026`.\n\n## Test suites\n\n| Suite | Kind | Covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PathGuardTest` | plain JVM | normalisation, traversal rejection, filename sanitising, root containment |\n| `SqlGuardTest` | plain JVM | single-statement rule, forbidden keywords, read/write verdicts, quote/comment-aware splitting |\n| `MimeTypesTest` | plain JVM | extension \u2192 MIME mapping, categories, inline-safe allow-list |\n| `TokenUtilTest` | plain JVM | token prefix, hash stability, constant-time compare |\n| `RateLimiterTest` | plain JVM | windowed allow/deny |\n| `StorageLayoutTest` | plain JVM | directory/file paths, id validation, temp files |\n| `BackupArchiveTest` | plain JVM | archive create/validate/extract, unsafe-entry rejection, size cap |\n| `ProjectStoreTest` | Robolectric | create/list/update/delete, duplicate names, restart persistence, per-project & global stats, delete confirmation |\n| `DatabaseManagerTest` | Robolectric | create/list, CRUD, parameterised queries, transactions/rollback, schema inspection, invalid SQL, export/import |\n| `FileStoreTest` | Robolectric | upload/download metadata + checksum, rename/move/delete, folders, traversal rejection, quota & size limits, missing/orphan detection |\n| `BackupManagerTest` | Robolectric | create/validate/restore, invalid archives, preserve-existing-data |\n| `TokenStoreTest` | Robolectric | create/verify/revoke, scoping, name validation, listing |\n| `DataApiTest` | Robolectric | end-to-end `DataApi.handle`: auth, project scoping, CORS preflight, `413`, `429`, `404`, `405` |\n\n`DataTestSupport` provides a per-test data root and resets the process-wide\n`SystemDatabase` singleton between tests (via reflection), so tests cannot leak\nstate into one another.\n\n## Results\n\nOn the development host used for this implementation the suite reports:\n\n```\n88 tests, 41 passed, 47 skipped, 0 failed\n```\n\nThe 41 pure-JVM tests run everywhere. The 47 Robolectric tests are **skipped on\nthis specific host** because it is a **Linux/aarch64** environment: Robolectric\nloads SQLite through a native runtime (`DefaultNativeRuntimeLoader`) and its\nlegacy `sqlite4java` fallback, and neither ships Linux/aarch64 binaries (they\nsupport x86_64 Linux/Windows and x86_64/arm64 macOS). On such a host Robolectric\ncannot open a database.\n\nRather than hide this, the Robolectric suites call\n`DataTestSupport.assumeRobolectricRuntimeSupported()` from `@Before`, which\nskips the test with a clear reason on unsupported platforms (JUnit\n`AssumptionViolatedException`). On a supported host (x86_64 Linux/Windows or\nx86_64/arm64 macOS) they run in full. See\n[platform-compatibility.md](platform-compatibility.md).\n\nTo confirm the system-under-test is not the cause, note that the same failure\noccurs before any of this project's code runs \u2014 the very first database open in\nRobolectric's own bootstrap triggers the native-runtime error.\n\n## Relationship to the specification\n\nThe suites cover the testing requirements from the feature specification:\n\n- **Database** \u2014 initialisation, project-specific DB creation, table creation,\n  parameterised queries, CRUD, transactions/rollback, schema inspection, invalid\n  SQL and errors, restart persistence, migrations (schema is versioned with\n  additive upgrade steps).\n- **Projects** \u2014 create/list/update/delete, cross-project isolation, duplicate\n  names/ids, confirmation requirement.\n- **Files** \u2014 upload/download and metadata (name/MIME/size/checksum), images and\n  documents, rename/move/delete, path traversal, missing files, size and quota\n  limits.\n- **Backups** \u2014 creation, validation, restore, invalid/incomplete archives,\n  preservation of existing data.\n- **API/Security** \u2014 unauthorized requests, cross-project access, invalid input,\n  oversized uploads (`413`), rate limiting (`429`), `404`/`405`, SQL-injection\n  attempts (via `SqlGuard` and parameter binding).\n\n## Lint / static analysis\n\n`sh gradlew :app:lintDebug` can be run for Android Lint. It is not part of the\ndefault `assembleDebug`/`test` path and was not required to build the debug\nAPK.\n\n## Ad network coverage\n\nThere are **no automated tests for the ad-network classes or the dashboard assets** (and no CI). The 88-test suite covers the data/storage platform only. Manual verification is required:\n\n1. Enable **Settings \u2192 Ad Network**, start the server, open `http://<phone-ip>:8090/` and complete setup with the phone-displayed code.\n2. Create a site with a real hostname, a placement, a campaign and an approved creative; fund the advertiser and approve the campaign.\n3. Serve a page from that hostname with the generated tag (or call `/serve` with matching `Origin`/`Referer`), then confirm `/serve` returns JSON and `/imp` returns `204`.\n4. Click through `/c` and confirm the redirect plus a click row in reports; replay a token and confirm it is recorded as invalid and not billed.\n5. Confirm no fill (no eligible campaign) returns `204`; confirm a wrong domain returns `403`; confirm the dashboard works with JavaScript on and shows the `<noscript>` panel with it off.\n\nThe ad dashboard is CSP-sensitive: static checks should confirm there are no inline `<script>`, `style=` attributes or `on*=` handlers in `assets/adnet/*`.\n"
  }
};